.TH "HTMLELEMENT.CONTAINERTIMING" "3JS" "October 26, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLElement.containerTiming \- HTMLElement: containerTiming property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]containerTiming\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]HTMLElement\f[R] interface marks the element as a container root
for observation in the Container Timing API.
.PP
The \f[CR]containerTiming\f[R] property reflects the value of the
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute.
Setting the property adds the attribute, making the element a container
root: if the element is already in the document, only subsequent paints
contribute to its timing.
.SH VALUE
A string.
This is the empty string (\f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]) if the attribute isn\(cqt
set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Logging the value of \f[CR]containerTiming\f[R]
In this example, adding the \f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute to the
\f[CR]<section>\f[R] element sets it up as a container root.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq id=\(dqhero\-section\(dq>
  \f[I]<!\-\- ... \-\->\f[R]
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
We can then read the property and log its value:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] el = document.getElementById(\(dqhero\-section\(dq);
console.log(el.containerTiming); \f[I]// \(dqhero\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
For examples of how to use the Container Timing API to get timing
information, see \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R].
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] HTML attribute
.IP \(bu 2
